{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FjDJCk6X8MWPpWariVsmHt","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient experimental temperature dependence Arrhenius NMR\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"The dynamics of liquid 1-ethyl-3-methylimidazolium acetate measured with implanted-ion $^8$Li $\\\\beta$-NMR\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1907.03684\"},{\"title\":\"link.aps.org\",\"url\":\"https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevB.83.094302\"},{\"title\":\"self diffusion coefficient study of liquid lithium\",\"url\":\"https://scholar.xjtu.edu.cn/en/publications/self-diffusion-coefficient-study-of-liquid-lithium/\"},{\"title\":\"li self diffusion in garnet type lisub7sublasub3subzr sub2subosub\",\"url\":\"https://research-portal.st-andrews.ac.uk/en/publications/li-self-diffusion-in-garnet-type-lisub7sublasub3subzr-sub2subosub/\"},{\"title\":\"ecs.confex.com\",\"url\":\"https://ecs.confex.com/ecs/imlb2014/webprogram/Paper35263.html\"},{\"title\":\"Self-diffusion coefficients of ionic liquids measured by multinuclear benchtop NMR\",\"url\":\"https://magritek.com/?p=21016\"},{\"title\":\"ecs.confex.com\",\"url\":\"https://ecs.confex.com/ecs/imlb2016/webprogram/Paper76763.html\"},{\"title\":\"link.springer.com\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00883553\"},{\"title\":\"s00231 011 0874 9\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00231-011-0874-9\"},{\"title\":\"Chinese Chemical Letters  2017, Vol. 28 Issue (2): 362-366   PDF\",\"url\":\"https://html.rhhz.net/zghxkb/20170237.htm\"}]\n\nThe search didn't turn up a direct experimental NMR measurement of liquid lithium's self-diffusion coefficient with Arrhenius parameters. It returned mostly related work.\n\n- **Classic experimental reference:** A 1968 paper by Murday and Cotts, \"Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium,\" appears in a reference list (Murday JS, Cotts RM (1968) Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium. J Chem Phys 48:4938–4945). The search did not show its method or fitted parameters. From memory, I believe it used NMR, but that isn't confirmed here.\n- **Other experimental data:** A 1994 paper by Feinauer, Majer and Seeger on 6Li and 7Li diffusivities is listed, but no content from it was retrieved.\n- **Simulation with Arrhenius fit:** A 2012 study in *Wärme- und Stoffübertragung* applied an EAM potential to liquid lithium and derived an Arrhenius equation from the simulation results. It reports that calculated self-diffusion coefficient is in good agreement with Murday's experiment results in atmosphere. Its pressure dependence is also noted: the liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient increases with temperature increasing and decreases with pressure increasing.\n- **Solid-state NMR, for context only:** Solid lithium 7Li T1 work gives a diffusion expression of the form D_SD = D10·exp(−Q1/RT)·[1+D21·exp(−Q21/RT)]. This is a solid-metal vacancy model, not a liquid Arrhenius fit.\n\nFor the actual numerical activation energy and prefactor, the Murday & Cotts (1968) paper is the primary source to check directly. The search did not return its contents.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9a11176f-3086-43ac-8358-e42037899d12","uuid":"dff75fcc-82bc-4bd5-87b7-15285da4be76","timestamp":"2026-11-01T08:41:30.401Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient experimental temperature dependence Arrhenius NMR","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01DvV7JRdnaWCqevhXsw3ZM3","content":[{"title":"The dynamics of liquid 1-ethyl-3-methylimidazolium acetate measured with implanted-ion $^8$Li $\\beta$-NMR","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1907.03684"},{"title":"link.aps.org","url":"https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevB.83.094302"},{"title":"self diffusion coefficient study of liquid lithium","url":"https://scholar.xjtu.edu.cn/en/publications/self-diffusion-coefficient-study-of-liquid-lithium/"},{"title":"li self diffusion in garnet type lisub7sublasub3subzr sub2subosub","url":"https://research-portal.st-andrews.ac.uk/en/publications/li-self-diffusion-in-garnet-type-lisub7sublasub3subzr-sub2subosub/"},{"title":"ecs.confex.com","url":"https://ecs.confex.com/ecs/imlb2014/webprogram/Paper35263.html"},{"title":"Self-diffusion coefficients of ionic liquids measured by multinuclear benchtop NMR","url":"https://magritek.com/?p=21016"},{"title":"ecs.confex.com","url":"https://ecs.confex.com/ecs/imlb2016/webprogram/Paper76763.html"},{"title":"link.springer.com","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00883553"},{"title":"s00231 011 0874 9","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00231-011-0874-9"},{"title":"Chinese Chemical Letters  2017, Vol. 28 Issue (2): 362-366   PDF","url":"https://html.rhhz.net/zghxkb/20170237.htm"}]},"The search didn't turn up a direct experimental NMR measurement of liquid lithium's self-diffusion coefficient with Arrhenius parameters. It returned mostly related work.\n\n- **Classic experimental reference:** A 1968 paper by Murday and Cotts, \"Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium,\" appears in a reference list (Murday JS, Cotts RM (1968) Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium. J Chem Phys 48:4938–4945). The search did not show its method or fitted parameters. From memory, I believe it used NMR, but that isn't confirmed here.\n- **Other experimental data:** A 1994 paper by Feinauer, Majer and Seeger on 6Li and 7Li diffusivities is listed, but no content from it was retrieved.\n- **Simulation with Arrhenius fit:** A 2012 study in *Wärme- und Stoffübertragung* applied an EAM potential to liquid lithium and derived an Arrhenius equation from the simulation results. It reports that calculated self-diffusion coefficient is in good agreement with Murday's experiment results in atmosphere. Its pressure dependence is also noted: the liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient increases with temperature increasing and decreases with pressure increasing.\n- **Solid-state NMR, for context only:** Solid lithium 7Li T1 work gives a diffusion expression of the form D_SD = D10·exp(−Q1/RT)·[1+D21·exp(−Q21/RT)]. This is a solid-metal vacancy model, not a liquid Arrhenius fit.\n\nFor the actual numerical activation energy and prefactor, the Murday & Cotts (1968) paper is the primary source to check directly. The search did not return its contents."],"durationSeconds":5.845513389,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01FjDJCk6X8MWPpWariVsmHt","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015NhZz8yRgfjGWhEDHh6kKm","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"liquid lithium density temperature correlation g/cm3 reference data\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"repo.qst.go.jp\",\"url\":\"https://repo.qst.go.jp/records/47902\"},{\"title\":\"LITHIUM (Li)\",\"url\":\"https://qedfusion.org/LIB/PROPS/PANOS/li.html\"},{\"title\":\"ntrs.nasa.gov\",\"url\":\"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19680018893/downloads/19680018893.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"repo.qst.go.jp\",\"url\":\"https://repo.qst.go.jp/records/47902/export/json\"},{\"title\":\"wulixb.iphy.ac.cn\",\"url\":\"https://wulixb.iphy.ac.cn/article/doi/10.7498/aps.55.76\"},{\"title\":\"Operational conditions:\",\"url\":\"https://www.chemeo.com/mid/22-277-a/Choline-chloride_Urea_lithium-chloride\"},{\"title\":\"Lithium chloride · LiCl\",\"url\":\"https://processconvert.pages.dev/substances/lithium-chloride\"},{\"title\":\"densite et temperature de cristallisation d une solution aqueuse de 22391\",\"url\":\"https://iifiir.org/fr/fridoc/densite-et-temperature-de-cristallisation-d-une-solution-aqueuse-de-22391\"},{\"title\":\"Contents - Index\",\"url\":\"https://fchartsoftware.com/ees/libr_help/hs400.htm\"},{\"title\":\"elogim.com\",\"url\":\"https://springerlink.bibliotecabuap.elogim.com/content/pdf/10.1361/105497198770341761.pdf\"}]\n\nI found one explicit empirical correlation for pure liquid lithium, plus a 1968 NASA correlation that didn't show its formula in the snippet.\n\n- **Explicit correlation (QED Fusion compilation):** Density in g/cm³ is ρ = 0.5082 − 1.0336×10⁻⁴·T* − 4.8279×10⁻¹⁰·T*² − 5.2853×10⁻¹²·T*³, with T* = T − 271.7. The page doesn't state the temperature unit, and 271.7 doesn't match lithium's melting point in either °C or K, so verify the units against the original reference before use. A rough check at 200 °C gives about 0.516 g/cm³, which seems plausible. The page cites a set of underlying references but doesn't name the primary source for this fit. [qedfusion.org](https://qedfusion.org/LIB/PROPS/PANOS/li.html)\n- **NASA correlation (NTRS 1968 report):** This report correlated liquid lithium density against temperature from a literature review plus experimental data, and extrapolated the properties to about 1600 K. It chose a linear polynomial form, because a cubic fit gave a density minimum near 1600 K that it couldn't physically justify. The snippet doesn't include the coefficients. [ntrs.nasa.gov](https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19680018893/downloads/19680018893.pdf)\n- **Not pure lithium:** The Pb–Li alloy correlation from Fusion Engineering and Design (2015) gives density as a function of temperature and Li concentration, so it doesn't apply to pure Li. [repo.qst.go.jp](https://repo.qst.go.jp/records/47902)\n\nFor a citable pure-lithium table, the NASA report is the most authoritative source I found, but I couldn't retrieve its coefficients. Searching for the report's density coefficients or a handbook table (e.g., Gschneidner's lithium data) would be the next step.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9a11176f-3086-43ac-8358-e42037899d12","uuid":"0bfa2cca-5642-40fc-b4e0-b57617ed2d93","timestamp":"2026-11-01T08:41:31.764Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"liquid lithium density temperature correlation g/cm3 reference data","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01JBXKo3bfaMpAUtCrwVsMdw","content":[{"title":"repo.qst.go.jp","url":"https://repo.qst.go.jp/records/47902"},{"title":"LITHIUM (Li)","url":"https://qedfusion.org/LIB/PROPS/PANOS/li.html"},{"title":"ntrs.nasa.gov","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19680018893/downloads/19680018893.pdf"},{"title":"repo.qst.go.jp","url":"https://repo.qst.go.jp/records/47902/export/json"},{"title":"wulixb.iphy.ac.cn","url":"https://wulixb.iphy.ac.cn/article/doi/10.7498/aps.55.76"},{"title":"Operational conditions:","url":"https://www.chemeo.com/mid/22-277-a/Choline-chloride_Urea_lithium-chloride"},{"title":"Lithium chloride · LiCl","url":"https://processconvert.pages.dev/substances/lithium-chloride"},{"title":"densite et temperature de cristallisation d une solution aqueuse de 22391","url":"https://iifiir.org/fr/fridoc/densite-et-temperature-de-cristallisation-d-une-solution-aqueuse-de-22391"},{"title":"Contents - Index","url":"https://fchartsoftware.com/ees/libr_help/hs400.htm"},{"title":"elogim.com","url":"https://springerlink.bibliotecabuap.elogim.com/content/pdf/10.1361/105497198770341761.pdf"}]},"I found one explicit empirical correlation for pure liquid lithium, plus a 1968 NASA correlation that didn't show its formula in the snippet.\n\n- **Explicit correlation (QED Fusion compilation):** Density in g/cm³ is ρ = 0.5082 − 1.0336×10⁻⁴·T* − 4.8279×10⁻¹⁰·T*² − 5.2853×10⁻¹²·T*³, with T* = T − 271.7. The page doesn't state the temperature unit, and 271.7 doesn't match lithium's melting point in either °C or K, so verify the units against the original reference before use. A rough check at 200 °C gives about 0.516 g/cm³, which seems plausible. The page cites a set of underlying references but doesn't name the primary source for this fit. [qedfusion.org](https://qedfusion.org/LIB/PROPS/PANOS/li.html)\n- **NASA correlation (NTRS 1968 report):** This report correlated liquid lithium density against temperature from a literature review plus experimental data, and extrapolated the properties to about 1600 K. It chose a linear polynomial form, because a cubic fit gave a density minimum near 1600 K that it couldn't physically justify. The snippet doesn't include the coefficients. [ntrs.nasa.gov](https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19680018893/downloads/19680018893.pdf)\n- **Not pure lithium:** The Pb–Li alloy correlation from Fusion Engineering and Design (2015) gives density as a function of temperature and Li concentration, so it doesn't apply to pure Li. [repo.qst.go.jp](https://repo.qst.go.jp/records/47902)\n\nFor a citable pure-lithium table, the NASA report is the most authoritative source I found, but I couldn't retrieve its coefficients. Searching for the report's density coefficients or a handbook table (e.g., Gschneidner's lithium data) would be the next step."],"durationSeconds":6.743551186,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015NhZz8yRgfjGWhEDHh6kKm","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
